WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.909
If some lenders had their way, you'd do all
your shopping on a credit card.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:04.909
Why?

00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:06.689
Because it makes them money.

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:07.689
Lots of it.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:10.660
And to try and entice you into keep spending,
they offer rewards.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.960
Some really, really nice rewards.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.480
But are those rewards really worth it?

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.010
Who's being rewarded?

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.000
You?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.990
Or your bank?

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.300
If you're careful, airline, hotel, and credit
card points can get you far.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:37.469
And some people spend a tremendous amount
of time seeing exactly how far that is.

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:38.469
This is Brad.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.920
And he's a bit of a ... Let's call him a credit
card connoisseur.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.749
I decided to head down to Murray Hill to check
out his massive card collection.

00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:49.210
This is a lot of credit cards.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.210
Walk me through.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.210
How many are here?

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.100
I think there is about 70, or 80 here.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.180
How many of these are open?

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.559
I think there is about 20 to 25 that are open
right now.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.999
How did you get into it?

00:00:59.999 --> 00:01:00.999
How did that process begin?

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:06.700
I got a letter in the mail, saying you can
get ... I think it was 50,000 Delta points

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.630
for spending $3,000 in three months.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:09.630
Okay.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.540
There was no fee in the first year.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.580
Yeah, I've gotten those.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:13.600
I always toss those.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.799
Yeah, well you should read them 'cause sometimes
you can see that if there is no fee, and you're

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:21.789
gonna spend that $3,000 in three months-
Yeah.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:24.259
If you're doing that anyway, it's free points.

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:27.909
Do you know how many points you've accumulated
over the last five years?

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:28.909
Yeah.

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:29.909
What is it?

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:32.390
It's probably between seven and ten million
points.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.969
Are you joking?

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:34.969
No.

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:36.880
How much time do you spend on this?

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.990
On just this, it's probably a couple hours
a week.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.109
I have a spreadsheet that I maintain.

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:47.759
And there is websites that will keep track
of your loyalty accounts.

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:51.990
But also there is kind of research that I'm
doing.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:57.080
The best general card anyone can get is just
2% cash back, no annual fee.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.080
Okay.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.509
At the end of the year, you get a check in
the mail.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:02.530
It's just free money.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.490
It's what you can get, and you don't have
to think about it at all.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.659
It seems like kind of exhausting.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:09.610
To someone else, it would seem like homework.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.720
But I kind of enjoy it.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.540
If someone wants to get into it, what would
you tell them?

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.540
What's the way to start up?

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.100
The very first thing I would tell them, is
just make sure your finances are in order.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.330
Don't carry a balance.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.940
Don't pay unnecessary interest.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.230
Don't ever carry a balance?

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.230
No.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.110
'Cause that's ... You're paying interest on
any balance that you're carrying.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.110
Right.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.110
That's money you're giving away.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.640
Treat it like a debit card.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.640
Okay.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.040
It's money that you're spending each month,
and you pay it off immediately.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.280
Okay.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.650
Managing 25 open cards can be exhausting.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.310
Done right, like Brad, you could get a lot
of free stuff.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.150
But it's also full of traps that could expose
you to expensive fees.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:54.810
Remember banks are in the business of making
money, and credit cards are one of their most

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.580
profitable activities.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.690
Here to tell us more about how banks usually
win is Bloomberg reporter Jenny Surane.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.230
Credit card companies, their ideal goal is
that you'll spend each month, and then you'll

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:10.620
pay off your bill.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:18.570
Or ideally actually you'll carry a balance,
and they can charge you that interest.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.950
When you're thinking about getting a credit
card, and using a credit card, interest is

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.300
one of the most important things to be paying
attention to.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.230
For instance, let's say you buy a TV for $1,000.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:32.070
Even if you pay off $999 of that bill, you
could still be charged interest on the whole

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.290
thing, even if you've only left a dollar on
your balance.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.871
And credit cards sometimes come with the highest
form of interest.

00:03:37.871 --> 00:03:42.460
If you're thinking about going and chasing
after crazy credit card rewards, you have

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.170
to be really responsible, and stay on top
of your payments.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:49.500
If you're very responsible, and meticulously
organized like Brad, then you're probably

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.500
set.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.040
If it's something where you're gonna be carrying
a balance, you probably should walk away.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.230
Yeah, chasing points requires a lot of time
and care.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.410
Just look at Brad.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:03.290
As the old saying goes, "Make sure the juice
is worth the squeeze."

